// The network read (engi: "perseid wasm fetch should go via perseid fetch").
//
// `http` names the TRANSPORT (the interface dials HTTP), `fetch` names the
// FUNCTION (the one operation: read a URL). The package+interface id -
// perseid:network/http@0.1.0 - is the capability a program's
// spec.capabilities declares and the host checks.
//
// THE DECLARED-OBLIGATION SHAPE, like reconcile's ensure: fetch declares
// that the program wants this URL read, returns nothing, and there is no
// result a guest could branch on - the response is performed by radiant and
// recorded ON THE PROGRAM'S OWN STATUS (status.fetches, keyed by URL,
// body capped and the cap recorded in the entry), where the program
// observes it next pass through the ordinary three-valued read.
//
// IDEMPOTENT-TRANSPARENT AROUND EPOCHS (engi's directive): the recorded
// response persists on the object - a re-declare of the same URL is a no-op
// while the record stands, and the program decides when the world matters
// by changing the URL it declares.
package perseid:network@0.1.0;

interface http {
  /// Declare that this program wants `url` read (GET) and the response
  /// recorded on its own status for the next pass to observe.
  fetch: func(url: string);
}

/// The world a wasm Perseid imports to use the network read. Trail links
/// this interface; the obligation is forwarded to radiant, which performs
/// the read and records the response on the Perseid's own status.
world perseid-network {
  import http;
}
